WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.285 --> 00:00:04.118
Who are you?

00:00:04.118 --> 00:00:06.900
We are from Mars, don't be afraid.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.013
We have children just like you on Mars.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.020
Human beings have contemplated

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:15.763
whether life exists beyond
Earth for thousands of years.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:19.693
Ancient one of Mars I call upon you.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:23.640
It's thought that there's a possibility

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.750
that simple microbial life might exist

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.200
within our solar system,

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.023
but not advanced complex life.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:31.840
Doesn't mean it doesn't exist,

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.970
it just means we don't expect to find it,

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:36.570
largely because the conditions for life

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.550
elsewhere in the solar system appear

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:41.863
to be extremely hostile to such life.

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:45.950
But since the early 90s,

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:48.040
technology has allowed
us to locate planets

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.460
outside of our solar system.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:54.160
They're known as exoplanets
and planet-hunting telescopes

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:55.860
like Kepler and TESS,

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.733
have discovered roughly
4,000 of them and counting.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.400
So we now understand
that majority of stars

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.370
will have planets.

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:07.920
So planets are ubiquitous.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.150
And because we just reach that sensitivity

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:14.150
that is allowing us to
detect those exoplanets,

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:16.863
this field is evolving very rapidly.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:19.800
And while they're too far away

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.910
to send a probe to even
the closest exoplanets,

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:25.480
a team at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.610
has been developing a method more powerful

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.563
than any telescope ever built.

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:32.990
It'll be able to give us
a close-up look at planets

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:36.180
and other objects thousands
of light-years away,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.460
find worlds that are similar to Earth,

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.173
and maybe even discover
signs of intelligent life.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.643
So that's what the Earth
people call a city?

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:46.803
How primitive.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.110
Look at all those buildings above ground.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:51.960
And it will be amazing discovery,

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.230
first of all to see that we are not alone

00:01:55.186 --> 00:01:58.150
and how life evolved in that system.

00:01:58.150 --> 00:01:59.710
So it's an enormous step

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.340
in our sort of philosophical understanding

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:03.690
who we are and where we go.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.480
Powerful telescopes, like
the Hubble Space Telescope,

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.890
Spitzer, Herschel and Keck Observatory

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.023
have photographed objects very far away.

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:23.080
This galaxy called The Sombrero Galaxy

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.483
is 28 million light-years from Earth.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.520
But even though known
exoplanets are as close

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:33.010
as four light-years, these
are the best images we have.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:36.740
All we really get are just
these tiny little blobs.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:38.720
They just look like fuzzy, little points

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.670
of light orbiting some
distance from the star.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:45.950
So we don't really see anything
that looks like a planet.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:48.380
In fact, if we didn't know
any better we might think

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:50.610
that it was just a very very faint star,

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:55.050
when in fact it's a very
very large planet reflecting

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.553
the starlight off of it.

00:02:57.597 --> 00:03:00.380
And of the 4,000 discovered exoplanets,

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.180
only about 50 of them have been imaged.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.080
So basically it's really really hard

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:06.413
to image an exoplanet?

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:10.590
It's really hard, but not impossible.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:14.170
And that's why we're aiming
to do that, you know,

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:16.270
that's why we're trying
to make that happen,

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:18.120
but you're right, it's very difficult,

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.510
it's certainly is not
beyond our capabilities

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:24.693
or won't be beyond our capabilities.

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.130
The majority of exoplanets

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.230
are found using the transit method,

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:34.350
a tiny dip in a star's
brightness when a planet passes

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.660
or transits in front of it.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.620
And it's that telltale drop off in light,

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.220
especially when it repeats
over several cycles

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:45.060
that tells us that this
is caused by a planet,

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.720
orbiting the star making regular transits

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.400
across its surface.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:50.800
Another common method

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.380
is looking at slight shifts
in the motion of a star

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:55.110
when there's a planet orbiting it.

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:59.010
This is known as the radial
velocity or wobble method.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:01.510
The planet exerts gravity on the star.

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:04.720
So what happens is that as the
planet comes around the star,

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.160
the star also kind of comes around

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:08.970
the system's center of mass.

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.640
And so the star has to move.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.070
Although both of these methods

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:15.690
allow planetary scientists
to make predictions

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:19.590
like the basic mass, size,
whether it's rocky or gaseous,

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:21.480
and distance to its host star,

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.403
it doesn't allow us to see it.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.180
In the best case scenario,

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.460
the light can be analyzed
with modern telescopes

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:33.000
to give hints as to whether
the planet is habitable.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.160
How can we even tell that
a planet is habitable

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:37.070
just by a fuzzy blob?

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:38.840
We certainly cannot.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:42.060
So the goal with these telescopes
is not to actually show us

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:43.980
what the planet looks like,

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:48.210
but it would allow us to
analyze the light coming

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:49.830
from the planet.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:50.663
The spectrum of light

00:04:50.663 --> 00:04:52.570
coming from the planet can be analyzed

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:55.670
to reveal the chemical
components of its atmosphere,

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:59.210
a much easier feat than
getting a real life image.

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:02.120
That's because in comparison to a galaxy,

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:05.110
exoplanets are dark and tiny.

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:08.630
Planets just reflect light
from their host star.

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:12.020
So even though a planet

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:16.990
may only be a few dozen
light-years down the road,

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:20.860
our chances of seeing it are much less

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:24.820
than seeing a bright
star that's even farther

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:29.820
or a bright galaxy that's
billions of light-years away.

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:33.713
It's just the nature of
the luminosities involved.

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:36.930
Even with the upcoming

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:38.720
James Webb Space Telescope,

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:42.200
which will be the most powerful
space telescope ever built,

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.873
the images won't give you
much more than fuzzy blobs.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:48.700
What we want is an image where
you can see color variations

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:52.250
that distinguish oceans,
continents, vegetation,

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:54.270
and even see lights at night.

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:56.620
That can determine not
just if it's habitable,

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:59.290
but show proof of advanced life.

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:01.160
But to image an Earth-sized exoplanet,

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.890
that's a hundred light years
away at a decent quality,

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:05.800
1000 by 1000 pixels,

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:06.900
you would need a telescope

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.870
that is 90,000 kilometers in diameter,

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:12.130
seven times larger than Earth.

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:15.230
And even if it were made of
a super lightweight material,

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:18.430
it would still weigh about
a trillion kilograms.

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:20.463
In other words, it's not possible.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:23.300
What we need to do instead
is rather than think

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:26.120
about even building a telescope,

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:30.150
we need to think about using nature itself

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:31.680
as a kind of telescope.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:34.440
And luckily nature gives
us a way to do that.

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.000
This was discovered by Albert Einstein,

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.280
and it's the idea that mass or gravity

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:43.230
can bend the path of light.

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:46.400
As long as that can happen,
then we can in theory,

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:48.550
go to where all those light rays

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:51.300
that are being bent by a mass converges,

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:52.840
and we can take an image.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:55.233
This is called gravitational lensing.

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:04.610
As long as we can choose the
right place to put a telescope,

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:08.170
we could in principle,
look back toward the sun,

00:07:08.170 --> 00:07:12.660
image the gravitational lens
from a distant exoplanet,

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:13.493
and for the first time,

00:07:13.493 --> 00:07:15.110
we would actually be
able to see continents.

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:19.160
We'd be able to see the
oceans and map the coastlines

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:23.963
of a Earth-sized planet, maybe
a hundred light-years away.

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:29.060
This is a very different
approach to making images

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:32.830
with a telescope that we
could build here on Earth,

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:35.610
but it may in fact be the
only way that we'd be able

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:40.480
to directly image the
surface of another planet.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:42.800
It may sound like science fiction,

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:45.323
but we've actually observed
this phenomenon before.

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:48.350
When light from a distant galaxy

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:50.420
is behind a massive foreground object,

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:52.300
from our perspective on Earth,

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:55.140
the mass of that foreground
object bends space time,

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:58.600
creating gravity and bending
the light around that object.

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:00.733
This forms a ring called an Einstein ring.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:04.580
To visually demonstrate this,

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:06.700
you can use a wine
glass to act as the lens

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:08.820
or a foreground object and a light bulb

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:10.850
that can act as the source.

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:13.000
If we the observer
looked through the center

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:15.883
of the base of the wine
glass, we see a ring of light.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:20.410
And in theory, we could
use computer software

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:23.473
to decode that ring of light
back into its original state.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:27.360
If you replace the wine glass
with the sun to bend the light

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:29.770
and send a telescope, now the observer,

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:32.380
to the point where the light converges,

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:34.300
you could then take images of the ring

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:36.900
and put it back together using software.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:38.450
The sun is massive enough

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:40.770
that it will amplify whatever
you're pointing it at,

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:43.100
by a factor of a hundred billion,

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.923
just like an astronomically
massive telescope.

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:50.630
That's why a team of scientists
led by Slava Turyshev

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:52.680
at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:55.823
are proposing a solar
gravitational lens mission.

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:01.610
Flying a small telescope to
the solar gravitational lens

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:05.650
will allow us to study those
objects in very fine details

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:07.750
and confirm the presence of life,

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:11.163
and study the evolution
of life on that exoplanet.

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:15.940
There are a few challenges
though, for this to work,

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:17.590
the telescope will need to be positioned

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:21.340
past the convergence point at
around 650 astronomical units

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:23.439
or AU from the Sun.

00:09:23.439 --> 00:09:27.070
One AU equals the distance
from the Earth to the Sun.

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:29.640
Neptune is at 30 AU for example.

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:32.260
And Voyager 1, the farthest manmade object

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:34.010
that was launched in the 70s,

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:36.653
is currently at about 150 AU.

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:41.710
So we have thought about how
we can reach those distances,

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:43.700
what technology do we have?

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:46.430
And essentially we realized
that using chemical propulsion

00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:49.960
is very challenging because
the fastest velocity

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:52.750
we were able to achieve
with chemical propulsion

00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:56.680
was achieved with the New
Horizons mission to Pluto

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:57.910
and New Horizons was able

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:01.730
to reach roughly three
astronomical units per year.

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:04.730
With this velocity, it
will take us a lot of time

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:06.720
to get to solar gravity lens.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:10.430
So, we realized that solar sailing

00:10:10.430 --> 00:10:12.430
offers a unique alternative.

00:10:12.430 --> 00:10:16.740
With solar sailing we are
using the solar photons,

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:20.130
solar light to gain a
very significant momentum

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:23.840
that will push our sail
craft to very high velocity.

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:25.570
Solar sailing has been successfully

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:27.800
demonstrated by The Planetary Society,

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:31.193
NASA and the Japanese
Aerospace Exploration Agency.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:36.000
If done in a way that Slava
and his team are proposing,

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:38.140
which involves flying close to the Sun,

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:41.623
the telescope will reach speeds
of roughly 25 AU per year.

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:44.080
So with those velocities,

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:46.540
we will be able to
reach solar gravity lens

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:50.210
within I would say 20 to 25 years.

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:51.790
That's the only way to do this.

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.990
The next challenge is
once you get to 650 AU,

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:00.040
there's no slowing down.

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:02.590
But this is where they get a lucky break.

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:04.980
It doesn't have just a single focal point,

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:06.710
it has a focal line.

00:11:06.710 --> 00:11:09.200
If you fly a spacecraft
towards the focal region

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:11.780
of the solar gravity lens,
we don't have to stop.

00:11:11.780 --> 00:11:14.090
Moving along that focal
line will still benefit

00:11:14.090 --> 00:11:16.600
from that large amplification.

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:17.870
Another challenge,

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:21.310
which current telescopes also
face when imaging exoplanets,

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:23.070
is that you have to point the telescope

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:26.610
at a very bright object,
in this case the Sun.

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:28.060
This creates a glare

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:29.980
which needs to somehow be blocked out

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:32.060
in order to see the planet.

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:34.360
We will use a very interesting technique

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:36.390
called a coronograph.

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:38.380
So the, or star shade,

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:41.100
the large instrument
that is, has to be flown

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.510
in front of our telescope in space,

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:45.220
and that coronagraph will allow us

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:48.480
to block the light from the parent star.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:51.360
And suddenly the very faint
light from the exoplanet

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:53.300
may present itself.

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:55.020
So it's a challenging technique

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:56.933
because the brightness mismatch.

00:11:58.400 --> 00:11:59.350
Fortunately,

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:01.470
coronagraphs are being
developed and in use

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:03.360
on many planet-hunting telescopes,

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:05.963
including the upcoming
James Webb Space Telescope.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:09.470
Finally, the telescope will be limited

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:11.150
to choosing a single target

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:13.450
since changing the angle by just a degree,

00:12:13.450 --> 00:12:15.590
could mean propelling it
billions of kilometers

00:12:15.590 --> 00:12:17.460
in the lateral direction.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:20.030
Usually people when talking
about solar gravity lens

00:12:20.030 --> 00:12:22.340
there is a significant limit,

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:25.780
because we currently are limited
by propulsion capability.

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:27.570
So we can think that we can fly

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:30.750
only towards one target essentially.

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:33.800
And so, I think about
solar gravity lens mission,

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:35.910
it's like any planetary exploration.

00:12:35.910 --> 00:12:37.990
If you fly towards a Saturn,

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:41.450
we're studying the
whole set of satellites,

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:44.830
the whole formula of satellites
around Saturn with Cassini.

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:46.850
If you fly around Jupiter with Galileo,

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:50.090
we're studying multiple
satellites of Jupiter.

00:12:50.090 --> 00:12:53.770
With the solar gravity lens,
flying towards a Trappist

00:12:53.770 --> 00:12:56.640
we can study every planet in that system.

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:57.510
The Trappist system

00:12:57.510 --> 00:12:59.910
is a star with seven planets orbiting it,

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:02.750
three of which are in the
theoretical habitable zone.

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:05.080
The sweet spot for a planet
to possess liquid water

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:07.910
on its surface and possibly support life.

00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:10.200
Pointing the telescope
at the Trappist system

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:13.030
could be a solution for
looking at multiple planets

00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:14.593
since they orbit the same star.

00:13:17.950 --> 00:13:20.410
Another way they're looking
to solve this is to build

00:13:20.410 --> 00:13:22.933
the telescopes cheaply
and send many of them.

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:26.460
These are just a few of the hurdles

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:27.960
that Slava and his team face,

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:30.360
but none of them are out of reach.

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:33.600
And what we realize is that
most of those technologies

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:34.630
already exist.

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:37.600
Some of them in a very high
technology readiness level,

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:38.540
some of them are yet

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:42.680
to reach a very reasonable
technology readiness level,

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:45.700
but all of them exist
in one form or another.

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:46.800
Most of this technology

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:49.620
is being developed
alongside other projects

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:52.650
that need to get far out into
interstellar space cheaply,

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:54.400
and in a reasonable amount of time.

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:57.190
So ultimately we will transfer

00:13:57.190 --> 00:14:01.000
those engineering developments to allow us

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.430
to explore further, more,
at a very affordable cost.

00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:07.440
Solar gravity lens is
our ultimate goal so far,

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:09.180
but looking at this we realize

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:11.280
that there are plenty
of synergistic efforts

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:15.330
within exploration community
and who benefits from them,

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:18.500
who benefit from those efforts
and we invite our colleagues

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:23.500
and friends to join us to
look at those technologies,

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:27.003
how we can use those
technologies in the near term,

00:14:27.003 --> 00:14:29.070
how those technologies will benefit us

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:31.380
in the next, five, 10 years.

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:33.420
Slava and his team
received phase III funding

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:36.120
from the NASA Innovative
Advanced Concepts Program,

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:38.570
and are developing methods
to reconstruct the image

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:39.920
from an Einstein ring,

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:42.610
as well as building instrument prototypes.

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:44.970
They hope to launch their
technology demonstration mission

00:14:44.970 --> 00:14:46.570
in the next three to five years.

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:51.680
So as of now, if you
could choose one target

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:53.660
for the solar gravitational lens,

00:14:53.660 --> 00:14:55.223
what would you choose and why?

00:14:56.960 --> 00:14:58.653
Oh man, only one?

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:03.090
Let's take a picture of
our closest neighbor,

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:04.530
so the Proxima, right?

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:06.890
So Proxima b, it's our neighbor.

00:15:06.890 --> 00:15:10.790
The closer the exoplanet
is, the larger its image

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:15.023
and therefore the more detail
we will be able to see.

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:22.060
The closest star to our solar
system, Proxima Centauri

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:26.420
is only 4.2 light years away,

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:28.380
and it has at least one,

00:15:28.380 --> 00:15:31.470
but probably two planets orbiting it.

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:36.260
I would love to use the SGL on that to see

00:15:36.260 --> 00:15:40.053
what the planetary system
next door looks like.

00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:45.130
It's possible that once
we get a close-up look

00:15:45.130 --> 00:15:47.400
at our nearest neighboring solar system,

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:49.540
we'll be able to find
out once and for all,

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:52.100
whether we're alone or at the very least,

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:55.730
get a detailed image of an
exoplanet for the first time.

00:15:55.730 --> 00:15:58.360
Slava and his team hope to
launch their first mission

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:01.603
to image an exoplanet in
the next 12 to 15 years.

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:06.530
So it's only in a short
40 years from today,

00:16:06.530 --> 00:16:10.830
you and I may discuss
the images of, you know,

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:13.080
life present on a different exoplanet.

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:17.020
And this is how the childhood dream

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:18.680
may realize for many of us.

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:21.340
So now we will have a clear understanding,

00:16:21.340 --> 00:16:23.880
yes we are not alone and oh by the way,

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:25.810
our life is excellent.

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:29.330
Or maybe we just have a
confirmation that those aliens use,

00:16:29.330 --> 00:16:32.170
you know, carbon fuel
as stupidly as we do.

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:34.070
Or maybe they come up
with something excellent

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:35.500
and we'll learn from them.

00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:38.870
But anyway, I think it's
exciting opportunity for us

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:42.380
to step above our daily
lives and think about

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:45.253
what our universe will
tell us about ourselves.

00:16:46.490 --> 00:16:48.490
So, while we're never gonna solve

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:52.220
every problem on Earth,
we can solve most of them.

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:57.220
And in addition to reducing
the pain of life on Earth,

00:16:57.400 --> 00:17:01.280
we can also increase the excitement

00:17:01.280 --> 00:17:03.260
of being alive in the first place.

00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:06.960
And that's what missions like
the solar gravitational lens

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:09.690
and these next generation
of space telescopes

00:17:09.690 --> 00:17:11.730
are allowing us to do.

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:14.310
And there's something just
fundamentally essential

00:17:14.310 --> 00:17:18.190
about learning about our
real true place in the cosmos

00:17:18.190 --> 00:17:19.740
and understanding how it works.

